 # Multicore competitor evidence inventory
 
-Snapshot: upstream `a99fc3b04a7ba6e620682df76cfba031a992a0bc`. 30 PR records: 16 open, 12 merged, 2 closed; 27 active competitors excluding ours. The current leaderboard contains 17 official scores.
+Snapshot: upstream `a99fc3b04a7ba6e620682df76cfba031a992a0bc`. 30 PR records: 16 open, 12 merged, 2 closed; 27 active competitors excluding ours. The leaderboard at this snapshot contains 17 official scores.
 
-**We have not completed a controlled performance comparison against another multicore PR yet.** The first Rust #7 attempt was invalid (launcher descriptor limit); a corrected rerun is pending; a disclosed portable/corrected Goose #31 comparison is planned. Same-source article-batching tests, 197-step conformance passes and single-core results do not prove superiority over those competitors or the remaining 25 active submissions.
+**The completed default-configuration Rust #7 study shows no stable overall lead.** All six attempts are retained; pairs 1 and 3 are valid, while pair 2 is invalid because of a VM clock jump. Combined app-plus-PostgreSQL CPU per success is 2.905% higher overall across the two valid pairs, with inconsistent tails. A separate `WORKERS=2 ARTICLE_BATCH=1` configuration screen against unchanged Rust is running; submitted defaults remain unchanged. Its acceptance requires at least 5% lower whole-target CPU per success and no paired p95/p99 regression in each of three valid, zero-error pairs. The disclosed portable/corrected Goose #31 reference remains unmeasured; 26 active competitors have no performance comparison by us. Same-source ablation, conformance passes and single-core results do not establish first place or superiority over them.
 
 The [official leaderboard](https://github.com/arjaythedev/multicore-server-challenge/blob/a99fc3b04a7ba6e620682df76cfba031a992a0bc/LEADERBOARD.md) measures dedicated 8-vCPU/16-GB x86_64 capacity with a remote generator and a passing 5-minute hold. PR head pins below identify source inventory; they do not independently prove which revision produced historical scores. Self-reported screens/holds are heterogeneous and are not sorted as a speed ranking. These claims have not been independently revalidated; absence of a result does not imply a slower implementation.
 
